From 14752454c9f4eacb69d18433a7987df0df8a0802 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:38:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08063/11362] The induce->plan horizon was 40 when the goals
 were at 61, and retention threw plannability away

TWO SHIPPED CHANGES on the live path the scored agent uses, both measured before being written.

(1) THE SEARCH HORIZON IS ONE SHARED RESOLVER, DEFAULTING TO 80.

`plan_in_model` and `_goal_satisfiability_check` both read `plan_max_depth_default()`. Sharing is
a SOUNDNESS requirement: the gate vetoes goals it cannot reach and justifies that veto in its own
comment by asserting the planner is bounded identically. Two literals drift on the next edit; one
resolver cannot. Deepen the gate alone and it certifies goals the planner then fails on.

Re-running the shipped gate and planner over 48 frozen candidates, sweeping ONLY max_depth:

  max_depth   clears dynamics   plan found   BOTH
         40                 9            2      0
         61                 9            6      3
        200                 9            6      3

Three tn36 engines that predict 17 of 17 held-out changing transitions EXACTLY, and already pass
the shipped trust gate, need a 61-action plan. At 40 they were discarded as
`goal_unreached_within_depth`. Cost is absent, not small: mean planner wall time 0.357s at 40,
0.339 at 61, 0.334 at 80, 0.355 at 200 -- flat, and lowest where the conversions happen, because
a search that succeeds stops early. max_nodes untouched at 20000; the same sweep moved 3
candidates from UNDECIDED to a firm `degenerate_goal_predicate`, i.e. the gate rejecting MORE
confidently. A horizon correction, not a threshold relaxation.

Why depth and not budget, measured: tn36's 32 changing root actions collapse to ONE distinct
successor and both search sites dedup by state key, so the tree is a PATH. On a path the node
budget buys depth one-for-one and only max_depth can stop the search.

80 rather than 61 because 61 is one game's exact requirement; pinning to it would overfit to the
only game that converts. Nothing above 61 bought anything. `CARNOT_ARC_PLAN_MAX_DEPTH=40`
restores the prior behaviour exactly.

(2) RETENTION BREAKS TIES ON PLANNABILITY -- AND MY FIRST VERSION WAS WRONG.

`is_best` was decided on `heldout_change_consistency` alone ~70 lines before `goal_check` ran, so
every round computed its plannability and discarded it. At the corrected horizon SIX tn36
candidates pass the shipped trust gate and only THREE plan; dynamics ranking chooses among them
by coin flip.

I first made plannability the PRIMARY key. `test_arc_engine_retention_best_round.py::
test_a_planned_return_reports_its_own_round_not_the_retained_one` -- round 1 good dynamics with a
constant-False goal, round 2 a weak mover with a reachable one, asserting the retained round
stays 1 -- failed. It was right and I was wrong: retention preserves the best DYNAMICS model
because that is what generalises, and a reachable goal on a weak engine is a plan to nowhere. The
measured conflict is among six candidates ALL at accuracy 1.0 -- a TIE, not a deficit. The rule
is `retention_signal >= best_signal`; nothing overrides a deficit. That conjunct is load-bearing
and mutation-proven: tightening it to `>` makes the rule unreachable on the tie that motivates
it, and the suite catches that.

THE PROMOTION TESTS WERE DECORATIVE UNTIL MUTATION TESTING SAID SO. They reimplemented the
predicate in the test file, so inverting the shipped expression and deleting its `retention_on`
guard both left the suite GREEN -- this project's own "prove each pattern is under test by
deleting it" rule, failed inside the change that cites it. Fixed by extracting
`should_promote_on_plannability` to module level as the single copy the tests call. Six mutations
now kill.

TWO EXISTING TESTS UPDATED, NOT DELETED -- both assert the depth LABEL logic and depended on the
default incidentally; each now pins max_depth=40 so the origin incident stays reproduced.

FRESHNESS: 7 ARTIFACTS ACKNOWLEDGED, NOT REBUILT, AND THE ARTIFACTS THEMSELVES SAID TO.
Four carry a rebuild_command and it WAS run against the changed source, then compared to the
published file leaf by leaf: 48501 + 20337 + 7403 + 1326 = 77,567 leaves, ZERO substantive
movement. The only differing keys were the 8 fields of each `original_timings_superseded_by_
rebuild` block, which the rebuild DELETES -- the destructive behaviour recorded at 0da7f75ad,
and the reason that block's own `the_preferred_path_next_time` names acknowledgement as the
correct move. So the rebuilds were treated as evidence and discarded; all four published
artifacts were restored and verified byte-identical to HEAD, and every results/ diff here is
+8 -0 with zero deletions. The other three register no rebuild_command, so their acknowledgements
say plainly that inertness is ARGUED from reachability and not proven, rather than borrowing the
rebuilt group's stronger claim.

SCOPE, HONESTLY. Every conversion is tn36. Of the other 36 stall candidates, 21 never had a
usable engine at all -- 13 predict no action changes anything, 8 are disproved at their own
dynamical fixed point -- and 8 are not valid Python. This unblocks a real and previously
invisible class; it does not fix induction, and banked levels are unchanged at 3/3.

Regression: 375 passed, 1 pre-existing failure (test_experiment_4865_fork_probe_audit), confirmed
by differential revert. Post-run `git status` showed tracked files modified that are NOT mine --
a concurrent session's security-audit work -- left untouched per the rule against reverting
unattributed paths. No results/** file was written by the test run.

